WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:05.040
Maddy: Communication is a key component
for any workplace, no matter what the

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:07.620
industry is, or the line of work involves.

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:13.110
But for communication to be effective,
everyone should know what and how they’re

00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:17.079
expected to communicate with each other
and who they should be reporting to.

00:00:18.049 --> 00:00:22.759
Phillip: Everyone should know what their
responsibilities are, or work will stop, timelines

00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:27.780
will be crashed, and goals won’t be achieved, as
people won’t know what they’re meant to be doing!

00:00:29.230 --> 00:00:31.979
Maddy: Effective communication
boosts productivity.

00:00:32.990 --> 00:00:37.459
When people are communicating, they’re
coordinating and they’re cooperating,

00:00:38.030 --> 00:00:42.310
meaning it’s much more likely that work is
being done on time to the right standard.

00:00:43.030 --> 00:00:48.130
People will feel more connected with both
their colleagues and their organisation, making

00:00:48.130 --> 00:00:54.179
them happier and more focused on their work,
therefore improving productivity and efficiency.

00:00:54.910 --> 00:01:00.230
Without effective communication people will
feel disconnected from each other, even if they

00:01:00.230 --> 00:01:02.990
know what their responsibilities and duties are.

00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:08.640
Phillip: Effective communication drives
engagement A business where people are

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:13.210
being effectively communicated with about
their responsibilities and priorities is

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:15.410
one that is probably hitting its targets.

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:20.809
But if efforts aren’t made to make
employees feel engaged, this will

00:01:20.849 --> 00:01:22.970
only last a certain amount of time.

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:28.730
By effectively communicating things like the
business’s purpose and ambitions, sharing

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:34.120
success stories, and encouraging social
communication, employees will feel more like

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:36.880
they’re part of something bigger than themselves.

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:43.650
Maddy: Effective communication encourages
loyalty, and it means that if information about

00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:48.800
the business and career possibilities within
the organisation, is being spread, people will

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:50.860
be more likely to want to stay for longer.

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:57.310
By encouraging loyalty and employee retention
a company can avoid potentially expensive

00:01:57.590 --> 00:01:59.820
and disruptive searches for new employees.

00:02:00.730 --> 00:02:05.509
Let’s consider for example that a company
has internal fast tracks to promote promising

00:02:05.510 --> 00:02:11.040
employees to management positions, but it doesn’t
communicate this fact about itself to its people.

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:16.510
They then won’t know that’s an option, so
they might look for opportunities elsewhere.

00:02:17.139 --> 00:02:21.189
But, if its existence is made clear
through effective communication, their

00:02:21.190 --> 00:02:24.209
retention of talented staff should improve.

00:02:25.209 --> 00:02:28.859
Phillip: Effective communication
creates a health ideas ecosystem.

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:34.110
If people and teams are able to easily
share their experiences, knowledge, and

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:38.430
ideas, that means there is a constant
information flow in the business.

00:02:39.070 --> 00:02:43.750
This is fertile soil for the growth of
novel ideas and approaches, helping the

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:46.079
business stay competitive and efficient.

00:02:47.030 --> 00:02:50.250
Maddy: Effective communication
helps with employee wellbeing.

00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:54.750
When people feel engaged and know
they’re being heard by their colleagues,

00:02:55.130 --> 00:02:56.829
this is positive for their wellbeing.

00:02:57.540 --> 00:02:59.700
No one likes to feel on their own or unheard.

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:06.040
Effective communication is a powerful tool
to resist that feeling, especially important

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:08.340
given how many people now work remotely.

00:03:09.370 --> 00:03:13.509
Phillip: Imagine for example, a company
that has a lot of remote workers, a

00:03:13.509 --> 00:03:15.470
model adopted during the pandemic.

00:03:16.270 --> 00:03:20.690
This has continued as it offers a lot of
benefits, meaning they can hire talent

00:03:20.740 --> 00:03:23.179
from a much wider geographic range.

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:29.370
But they’ve noticed that some employees are
talking about feeling isolated and lonely.

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:35.000
So, a dedicated social channel is set up
on the company’s internal instant messaging

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:40.000
platform to encourage people to have those
casual “get to know you” conversations

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:42.040
that would happen naturally in an office.

00:03:42.770 --> 00:03:46.700
This helps people feel less lonely
and improves employee wellbeing.

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:52.309
Maddy: We can see that there are many ways that
effective communication is of great importance

00:03:52.309 --> 00:03:57.590
to a business and that it is essential for
building and managing working relationships.

00:03:58.060 --> 00:04:03.950
Without it, organisations, and employees within
them will struggle to reach their full potential.

00:04:04.599 --> 00:04:10.509
We can also see that effective communication
impacts a business at all levels, helping with

00:04:10.510 --> 00:04:16.740
decision making, encouraging innovation, and
keeping a business competitive, alongside helping

00:04:16.740 --> 00:04:19.730
to maintain a productive and engaged workforce.

